# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Easiest path — zero manual secrets: `npm run up` (or `sh scripts/up.sh`)
# generates strong values for the three secrets below into .env on first run,
# then starts the stack. It never overwrites a value you've already set.
#
# Local development: `npm run docker:dev` supplies safe defaults (loopback-only
# ports + dev secrets) via docker-compose.dev.yml — copy nothing.
#
# Production (`docker compose up` / `npm run docker:prod` / Coolify): the base
# compose has NO secret defaults, so you MUST set these (use `npm run up`, a
# .env file, or your platform's env). Postgres and the sandbox-controller are
# not published to the host — only the platform port is.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Postgres password (compose builds DATABASE_URL from it). Required in prod.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Shared secret between the platform and the sandbox-controller. The controller
# has Docker-socket (root-equivalent) access and REFUSES TO BOOT without a
# non-default value in production. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# CONTROLLER_SECRET=

# Root of trust for encrypting provider API keys at rest. MUST be set in
# production: it lives OUTSIDE the database, so a DB leak alone cannot decrypt
# stored secrets. Must be 64 hex characters. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# If unset, production REFUSES TO BOOT (fail-closed). In dev — or in prod with
# ALLOW_DB_MASTER_KEY=true — Capka falls back to an insecure DB-stored key.
# CAPKA_MASTER_KEY=

# OPTIONAL hardening for the first-run setup wizard. Leave it UNSET for a normal
# install — first-run is frictionless and whoever runs setup becomes admin. Set
# it for a public, internet-reachable deploy where a stranger might race you to
# the setup page: then claiming the admin account requires this token, which the
# wizard reads from a #token=… setup link (printed by scripts/up.sh when set) or
# you paste once. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# SETUP_TOKEN=

# Only needed when running the app OUTSIDE docker-compose (which auto-configures
# it). Example: postgresql://Capka:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/Capka
# DATABASE_URL=

# Public origin of the app (scheme + host, no trailing slash). Optional: if
# unset, Capka derives it per-request from proxy headers (X-Forwarded-Proto /
# Host), so localhost and most reverse proxies work with no config. Set it
# explicitly in production behind a proxy — it's the non-spoofable source of
# truth for auth callbacks and absolute links. Example: https://capka.example.com
# PUBLIC_URL=

# Sandbox isolation runtime. Default `runc` — standard Docker isolation; boots on
# any host with no extra setup. For UNTRUSTED or MULTI-TENANT code, opt into
# gVisor: install it on the host (`sudo sh scripts/install-gvisor.sh`) and set this
# to `runsc`. The secure profile is then FAIL-CLOSED — the controller refuses to
# boot if gVisor isn't registered on the daemon (it never silently downgrades), so
# leave this at `runc` unless you've installed gVisor. See the README section
# "gVisor (opt-in hardening)".
# SANDBOX_RUNTIME=runc

# Sandbox internet access is normally managed in the admin panel (Security →
# Internet access). This env flag is the deployment-level gate; it defaults to
# true. Set it to false to hard-forbid sandbox egress regardless of the in-app
# toggle (for untrusted/multi-tenant deployments).
# SANDBOX_ALLOW_NETWORK=true

# Default-deny egress with a host allowlist (opt-in; blank keeps today's
# behaviour, which is the public internet minus the private ranges).
#
# Set it and a sandbox with network access can reach ONLY these hosts, through the
# egress-proxy service: it speaks HTTP CONNECT, resolves each host itself, and
# refuses everything else. Plain http:// is not reachable at all in this mode, and
# neither is DNS — the proxy does the resolving.
#
#   example.com        that host, port 443
#   *.example.com      its subdomains (NOT the apex — list both if you want both)
#   example.com:8443   that host on that port only
#
# Choose narrowly. Allowlisting a host that fronts other origins (a CDN apex) also
# allowlists them, and allowing github.com or a package registry leaves a route out
# for anything that can post to it.
# SANDBOX_EGRESS_ALLOW=pypi.org,files.pythonhosted.org,registry.npmjs.org

# The egress proxy's address on the sandbox network, and the subnet it comes from.
# Fixed on purpose: each sandbox's firewall pins the address it resolved when IT
# started, so a proxy that comes back on a different address leaves every running
# sandbox with no way out and nothing in the logs. Change these only on a subnet
# collision with a host route, and change both — the IP must be inside the subnet.
# SANDBOX_EGRESS_SUBNET=10.201.7.0/24
# SANDBOX_EGRESS_PROXY_IP=10.201.7.2

# Host folder mounts: when the admin enables folder access (Settings, off by
# default), the agent can bind-mount SERVER folders into its sandbox. This
# optional perimeter restricts which host paths are mountable to subpaths of the
# listed roots (colon-separated), on top of the built-in system-path denylist.
# Unset → any non-system path is allowed, with the admin confirmation as the gate.
# Recommended for multi-admin deployments. See SECURITY.md "Host folder mounts".
# SANDBOX_MOUNT_ALLOW=/srv/shared:/mnt/nas

# Pin the Capka image version (platform/controller/sandbox) for deliberate
# upgrades. Unset → `latest`. Set to a published release tag, e.g. v0.2.0, then
# `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d` to upgrade. See docs/UPGRADE.md.
# CAPKA_VERSION=

# Host port the platform is published on (container port is always 3000). Change
# it only if another app on this box already uses 3000; then point your domain
# routing at the new port. Unset → 3000.
# PLATFORM_PORT=3000

# Interface the platform port binds to. Default 0.0.0.0 (all interfaces) so it
# works out of the box. Set 127.0.0.1 to bind loopback-only when a reverse proxy
# fronts the app (the proxy reaches it via localhost) — recommended on hosts
# where Docker publishes past the firewall (UFW).
# PLATFORM_BIND=0.0.0.0

# ── Sandbox tuning (optional; the code defaults are shown) ──────────────────
# Per-sandbox RAM in MB. The sandbox /tmp + /opt/mcp tmpfs are charged to this
# same cgroup, so keep headroom above what the code needs — raising
# SANDBOX_TMP_MB without raising this makes the container OOM sooner, not later.
# SANDBOX_MEMORY_MB=512
# CPU cores per sandbox; fractional values are allowed (e.g. 0.5).
# SANDBOX_CPUS=1.0
# Maximum processes/threads per sandbox. gVisor consumes part of this budget;
# values below 128 can break image/document rendering. Default: 256.
# SANDBOX_PIDS_LIMIT=256
# Max concurrent sandboxes (running containers) per user. Past the cap the least
# recently used one is evicted. Budget host RAM as this × SANDBOX_MEMORY_MB.
# MAX_SESSIONS_PER_USER=5
# Idle time before a sandbox CONTAINER is stopped (files + workspace row survive,
# revived on the next tool call). Milliseconds. Default 15 min.
# SANDBOX_IDLE_TTL_MS=900000
# A background job keeps its sandbox alive past the idle cutoff: it holds a lease
# of this length, renewed on every check_job. Milliseconds. Default 1 hour.
# SANDBOX_BUSY_LEASE_MS=3600000
# Ceiling on that hold, counted from when the job took the lease — a stuck job
# can't keep a container forever. Milliseconds. Default 6 hours.
# SANDBOX_BUSY_MAX_MS=21600000
# How long a WORKSPACE may go unused before its files are deleted (row + disk) —
# the real "how long do my files live" knob. Milliseconds. Default 30 days.
# WORKSPACE_TTL_MS=2592000000
# Grace for TRULY orphaned dirs (no DB row) before the sweep removes them.
# Milliseconds. Default 1 hour.
# GC_GRACE_MS=3600000

# Database history retention. Cleanup runs once daily in bounded batches and is
# coordinated across replicas. Set any *_DAYS value to 0 to keep that table
# indefinitely. Defaults: completed/failed/cancelled tasks 30 days; finalized
# usage ledger 365 days; governance audit log 365 days. Live tasks, pending
# budget holds, and turns awaiting an answer/approval are never removed.
# TASK_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# USAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# DB_RETENTION_BATCH_SIZE=1000

# Turnkey HTTPS: set DOMAIN and `npm run up` brings the stack up behind Caddy
# with an automatic Let's Encrypt cert. Requires public DNS for DOMAIN pointing
# at this host and ports 80+443 free. Example:
# DOMAIN=capka.example.com

# OPTIONAL ACME account email. When set (only relevant with DOMAIN), Caddy also
# enables ZeroSSL as a fallback certificate issuer — useful because the free
# <ip>.sslip.io hostnames share one Let's Encrypt rate limit that can be briefly
# exhausted. Unset ⇒ Let's Encrypt only. Example:
# ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com
# NOTE: the install scripts (up.sh) turn this into Caddy config. On a plain
# `docker compose up` or Coolify deploy, set it by writing the line yourself:
#   mkdir -p data/caddy/conf.d && echo 'email you@example.com' > data/caddy/conf.d/email.caddy

# OPTIONAL host Docker socket path for the socket-proxy bind. Leave unset for a
# normal (rootful) daemon. Set it for ROOTLESS Docker, whose socket lives under
# your user runtime dir — otherwise the sandbox controller can't reach Docker.
# See SECURITY.md. Example:
# DOCKER_SOCKET=/run/user/1000/docker.sock

# OPTIONAL agent tracing over standard OTLP. Unset ⇒ off, with zero overhead.
# Any OpenTelemetry backend works (Langfuse, Phoenix, Grafana Tempo, Jaeger,
# Datadog, or an OTel Collector) — we emit plain OTLP and depend on none of them.
# Exports the turn tree only (turn → LLM calls → tool calls → sandbox/MCP work),
# never HTTP routes or DB queries. HTTP/protobuf and http/json are supported;
# gRPC is not. All standard OTEL_* vars apply (sampler, batch sizes,
# OTEL_SDK_DISABLED, OTEL_TRACES_EXPORTER=none, OTEL_SERVICE_NAME).
# Token counts ride the turn span as `capka.usage.*`; a backend's NATIVE usage/cost
# graphs stay empty, because ai@6 emits no token attributes of its own. Spend
# reporting stays in /settings/usage.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
#
# Langfuse Cloud example. The x-langfuse-ingestion-version header is not
# optional in practice: without it ingestion can lag by up to 10 minutes.
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://cloud.langfuse.com/api/public/otel
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=Authorization=Basic <base64(pk-lf-…:sk-lf-…)>,x-langfuse-ingestion-version=4

# PROMPTS AND DOCUMENTS ARE NOT EXPORTED unless you turn this on. Off, traces
# carry only structure: durations, models, token counts, tool names, error
# CATEGORIES. On, they also carry prompts, completions, tool arguments and
# results, and sandbox commands — i.e. your users' work.
# Sending that to a host other than this one requires BOTH flags; with only the
# first, content is force-disabled and the boot log says so.
# CAPKA_TELEMETRY_CONTENT=true
# CAPKA_TELEMETRY_CONTENT_REMOTE=true

# Cost in USD is NOT exported by default: the usage ledger in Postgres is this
# deployment's money truth (it holds pending reservations and shared-vs-own-key
# spend), and a second dollar figure in a tracing backend would be a second
# answer to the same question. Enable only if you would rather read cost there.
# CAPKA_TELEMETRY_COST=true

# Span-name prefixes to export; `*` exports everything. Registering a tracer
# provider also wakes Next.js's own request spans, which the default excludes.
# CAPKA_TELEMETRY_SPAN_PREFIXES=capka.,ai.
# Extra attribute keys (or `prefix.` forms) to allow past the export allowlist.
# The GenAI conventions are still experimental; this keeps you unblocked between
# releases. Your responsibility — the content gate still applies.
# CAPKA_TELEMETRY_EXTRA_ATTRIBUTES=ai.settings.output

# Step at which a long tool loop is told to stop calling tools and write its
# answer, so a bulk run wraps up instead of being cut off mid-tool at the cap.
# Unset ⇒ five below the agent step cap; clamped to the cap.
# FORCE_TEXT_AFTER_STEPS=20

# Hard ceiling on tool-calling steps in one turn, after which the run stops.
# Unset ⇒ 25. Raise it for legitimate bulk work; FORCE_TEXT_AFTER_STEPS follows it.
# MAX_AGENT_STEPS=25

# Wall-clock ceiling for one turn, tool calls included. Unset ⇒ 20 minutes. Raise
# it for heavy sandbox work (batch conversions, long scrapes, transcoding); a
# silent provider is caught far sooner by STREAM_IDLE_SECONDS regardless. This also
# bounds how long ONE run can hold a worker slot, and there are only
# WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENCY of them instance-wide with no per-user fairness — so raise
# that alongside this, or a few heavy turns make everyone else wait a full deadline.
# TASK_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=20

# Fraction of that budget after which the turn stops calling tools and writes its
# answer — the clock-side twin of FORCE_TEXT_AFTER_STEPS, so a run that is going to
# time out lands on a real reply instead of being cut off mid-tool. Unset ⇒ 0.8.
# WRAP_UP_AFTER_FRACTION=0.8

# ── Tool output (optional; the code defaults are shown) ─────────────────────
# Per-call cap on a tool's text output, in characters. The single biggest lever on
# cost: an uncapped result is persisted and re-sent on every later step.
# MAX_TOOL_OUTPUT_CHARS=30000
# Total tool output one whole TURN may produce. Unlike the mid-turn trim this holds
# no matter what the provider reports about usage.
# MAX_TURN_TOOL_OUTPUT_CHARS=400000
# Line budget for a single file read.
# MAX_TOOL_OUTPUT_LINES=1500

# ── Streaming and workers (optional; the code defaults are shown) ───────────
# Silence on the wire before a stream counts as stalled, in seconds. A retry gets
# twice this window once, because a long reasoning phase looks the same as silence.
# STREAM_IDLE_SECONDS=60
# How many times one turn re-streams after a stall or transient error before it
# reports the provider unresponsive.
# MAX_STREAM_RECOVERIES=3
# Turns this instance runs at once. There is no per-user fairness, so read this
# together with TASK_TIMEOUT_MINUTES before raising either.
# WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENCY=3
# Postgres pool size for the platform. Keep it comfortably above
# WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENCY, since web requests draw from the same pool.
# PG_POOL_MAX=20
# Last resort: `false` stops asking OpenAI-compatible endpoints to report token
# counts on a stream. Only for a gateway that breaks on the parameter without
# saying so — the cost of switching it off is turns billed as zero.
# CAPKA_STREAM_USAGE=true

# ── Workspace retention (optional; the code defaults are shown) ─────────────
# Background-job log directories kept per workspace, and the cap on one log in MB.
# JOBS_KEEP_DIRS=20
# JOB_LOG_CAP_MB=10
# Rendered view_file directories kept per workspace.
# VIEW_KEEP_DIRS=4
# Captured tool-output files kept per workspace, and the cap on one file in MB.
# OUTPUT_KEEP_FILES=5
# OUTPUT_FILE_CAP_MB=10

# ── MCP connectors (optional; the code defaults are shown) ──────────────────
# Cap on one MCP media payload, in bytes.
# MAX_MCP_MEDIA_BYTES=5242880
# Cap on one MCP tool description, in characters.
# MAX_MCP_TOOL_DESC_CHARS=1024
# Comma-separated connector names whose tools always load eagerly instead of being
# deferred behind find_tool. Unset ⇒ none are pinned.
# MCP_ALWAYS_LOAD=
# Connector tool schemas hide behind find_tool once they exceed this share of the
# context window (percent), capped at this many tokens.
# MCP_DEFER_TOKEN_PCT=10
# MCP_DEFER_TOKEN_MAX=8192

# Deprecated alias for PUBLIC_URL, kept working for deployments that predate it.
# Set PUBLIC_URL instead; if both are set, PUBLIC_URL wins.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=

# ── Optional features and telemetry naming ─────────────────────────────────
# Service name reported on OTLP spans.
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=capka
# Lets users import a claude.ai / chatgpt.com share link into a chat. Off unless set.
# CAPKA_SHARE_IMPORT=true
